Cyde Weys <vze23tnc@verizon.net> writes:
>     	Anyone know what the difference between M1_SWIM and M1_AMPHIBIOUS are 
> from the file src/mondata.h?  Their descriptions are the following ...
> 
> 
> #define M1_SWIM         0x00000002L     /* can traverse water */
> #define M1_AMPHIBIOUS   0x00000200L     /* can survive underwater */
> 
> Is there a creature that is amphibious but cannot swim?  I.e. some sort of 
> unbreathing creature that doesn't like water and can't move in it but can 
> survive because it doesn't need to breathe?  Anyone got any specific 
> examples of this?  Thanks!

Amphibious is _not_ the same as unbreathing; amphibious monsters still
require oxygen, but can obtain it (possibly magically) from water as
well as air. A simple search of monst.c would tell you that the only
amphibious non-swimming monsters in the game currently are floating
eyes and Juiblex; there are rather more swimming non-amphibious
creatures (ie, that can move fine on the surface but not for any
length of time underwater). From intr-343.txt:

amphibiousness
  acquired by
    Polymorphing into a floating eye, water elemental, newt, (baby)
    crocodile, or any ;.
  effects
    You can enter water safely.
    Eels' drowning attacks will not drown you.
    Fog clouds' moisture attacks will not harm you.

swimming
  acquired by
    Polymorphing into a gremlin, water nymph, woodchuck, water elemental,
    rust monster, water troll, newt, (baby) crocodile, or any of S;.
  effects
    You can move through water on the surface and without hindrance.

(In addition to the polymorphables, water demons, the mail daemon,
Medusa, and Orion can also swim, the latter two additionally being
amphibious.)

Note that the effects of amphibiousness are a subset of those of
unbreathing.

